topics.conceptExplanation:
### 1. Simple Definition
A class template is a blueprint for creating classes that manage data of unspecified types. Standard library containers like `std::vector<T>`, `std::map<K, V>`, and `std::pair<T1, T2>` are class templates.

### 2. Why It Is Needed
Data structures like stacks, queues, and balanced trees manage storage layout and access semantics (e.g. LIFO or FIFO) independently of what type is stored. Writing a `StackOfInts`, `StackOfDoubles`, and `StackOfStrings` violates DRY principles and creates massive technical debt. A single class template `Stack<T>` solves this for all types.

### 3. How It Works Internally
When a class template is declared, the compiler records the class layout template. When you instantiate `Stack<int>`, the compiler generates a complete class definition with `int` substituted for `T`. Only member functions that are actually called in code are instantiated, minimizing unnecessary bloat.

### 4. Syntax
```cpp
template <typename T>
class MyContainer {
    T m_data;
public:
    MyContainer(T data) : m_data(data) {}
    T getData() const;
};

// Out-of-line member function definition
template <typename T>
T MyContainer<T>::getData() const {
    return m_data;
}
```

### 5. Real-World Analogy
A standard cargo shipping container has ISO-standardized exterior corner castings, dimensions, and locking mechanisms so cranes and ships can handle it. The interior can hold automobiles, wheat, or computers. The container class is the physical steel shell; the cargo type is `T`.

### 6. Practical Use Cases
- Generic data structures: Stacks, queues, linked lists, circular buffers.
- Smart wrappers: Optional values (`std::optional<T>`), expected results (`std::expected<T, E>`).
- Key-value associations: `Pair<K, V>` or custom multi-attribute tuples.

### 7. Advantages and Limitations
- **Advantages:** Complete reuse of complex data structure logic, full type safety, no boxing/unboxing.
- **Limitations:** Out-of-line member function syntax can become verbose; requires template header visibility.

### 8. Common Mistakes
- Omitting `<T>` when defining member functions outside the class body (e.g., writing `T MyContainer::getData()` instead of `T MyContainer<T>::getData()`).

